template template Filling text and HTML templates with values, with automatic escaping for safety. Text A template is text with holes in it; every &#123;&#123;name&#125;&#125; is filled with the value of that name from a "context". A context is a map from name to value. If the output is HTML and the values came from a user, always use RenderHTML. It escapes the dangerous characters such as &lt;, so that nobody can inject code into your page. This website is built with this package. Use it for emails, notifications, generated configuration files and small HTML fragments. For whole web pages with components and styles, the layout language of Salam is the better tool; for formatting numbers and dates into a sentence, fmt is shorter. What the language has, and what it does not  Only three tags: &#123;&#123;name&#125;&#125;, &#123;&#123;#if name&#125;&#125; with an optional &#123;&#123;else&#125;&#125; and a closing &#123;&#123;/if&#125;&#125;, and the comment &#123;&#123;! ... &#125;&#125;. There are no loops, filters or dotted paths: build repeated parts in Salam and put the result in the context, as the HTML example does. The context holds only strings. Turn numbers and bools into text before you put them. Values are inserted as they are and never scanned again, so a value that contains &#123;&#123; cannot inject a tag. RenderHTML escapes &amp; &lt; &gt; " '. That makes a value safe as page text and inside a quoted attribute, but not inside a &lt;script&gt; block or as a link address: a value such as javascript:... passes through unchanged.   Fill a plain text message The common job is a message with a few names in it: an email, a notification or a receipt. Put every value in the context with put, then call Render. The same context can be filled again and rendered a second time, so one template serves every customer. A section wrapped in &#123;&#123;#if key&#125;&#125; ... &#123;&#123;/if&#125;&#125; appears only when the key has a value, and &#123;&#123;! ... &#125;&#125; is a comment that never reaches the output. template-guide-email.salamCopyimport template  func main:     letter := &quot;Hello {{name}},

Your order {{order}} has shipped.
{{#if tracking}}Track it here: {{tracking}}
{{/if}}{{! the tracking line is optional }}
Thanks,
{{shop}}&quot;      mut ctx := template.NewContext()     defer ctx.Free()     ctx.Put(&quot;name&quot;, &quot;Sara&quot;)     ctx.Put(&quot;order&quot;, &quot;#1042&quot;)     ctx.Put(&quot;tracking&quot;, &quot;https://post.example/T-77&quot;)     ctx.Put(&quot;shop&quot;, &quot;Kooche Books&quot;)     println template.Render(letter, ctx)     println &quot;-----&quot;     ctx.Put(&quot;name&quot;, &quot;Reza&quot;)     ctx.Put(&quot;order&quot;, &quot;#1043&quot;)     ctx.Put(&quot;tracking&quot;, &quot;&quot;)     println template.Render(letter, ctx) endOutputHello Sara,  Your order #1042 has shipped. Track it here: https://post.example/T-77  Thanks, Kooche Books ----- Hello Reza,  Your order #1043 has shipped.  Thanks, Kooche Books  Conditions and else &#123;&#123;#if key&#125;&#125; takes an optional &#123;&#123;else&#125;&#125; branch, and the blocks can be nested. A key counts as false when it is missing, empty, "false" or "0"; any other text, such as "yes" or "3", counts as true. The context only holds strings, so a number or a bool has to be turned into text first, for example with str.FromInt. template-guide-conditions.salamCopyimport template  func badge(user: str, admin: str, unread: str): str:     mut ctx := template.NewContext()     defer ctx.Free()     ctx.Put(&quot;user&quot;, user)     ctx.Put(&quot;admin&quot;, admin)     ctx.Put(&quot;unread&quot;, unread)     ret template.Render(&quot;{{user}}{{#if admin}} [admin]{{/if}}: {{#if unread}}{{unread}} new{{else}}no new{{/if}} messages&quot;, ctx) end  func main:     println badge(&quot;sara&quot;, &quot;true&quot;, &quot;3&quot;)     println badge(&quot;reza&quot;, &quot;false&quot;, &quot;0&quot;)     println badge(&quot;mina&quot;, &quot;&quot;, &quot;12&quot;)     println badge(&quot;ali&quot;, &quot;yes&quot;, &quot;&quot;) endOutputsara [admin]: 3 new messages reza: no new messages mina: 12 new messages ali [admin]: no new messages  Safe HTML from user data A page is usually built from small pieces. Render each row with RenderHTML, so that whatever a visitor typed is escaped, and then place the finished rows into the outer layout with Render. If you used RenderHTML for the outer step too, the rows would be escaped a second time and the browser would show the tags as text. template-guide-page.salamCopyimport template  struct Book:     pub title: str = &quot;&quot;     pub author: str = &quot;&quot; end  func main:     mut books := Vector {} as Vector&lt;Book&gt;     defer books.Free()     books.Push(Book { title = &quot;Salt &amp; Ink&quot;, author = &quot;N. Karimi&quot; })     books.Push(Book { title = &quot;&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;&quot;, author = &quot;a visitor&quot; })      row := &quot;&lt;li&gt;{{title}} by {{author}}&lt;/li&gt;
&quot;     mut rows := &quot;&quot;     mut item := template.NewContext()     defer item.Free()     each book in books:         item.Put(&quot;title&quot;, book.title)         item.Put(&quot;author&quot;, book.author)         rows += template.RenderHTML(row, item)     end      mut page := template.NewContext()     defer page.Free()     page.Put(&quot;heading&quot;, &quot;New books&quot;)     page.Put(&quot;items&quot;, rows)     layout_text := &quot;&lt;h1&gt;{{heading}}&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
{{items}}&lt;/ul&gt;&quot;     println template.Render(layout_text, page) endOutput&lt;h1&gt;New books&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;Salt &amp;amp; Ink by N. Karimi&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;alert(1)&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt; by a visitor&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; The rule to remember: escape a value exactly once, at the moment it is put into HTML. Text that you already escaped, or HTML that your own program built, goes in with Render

os os Files, directories, system commands, program arguments and environment variables. System and tools The os package is the simplest way to talk to the system: whole-file reads and writes, folders, shell commands, the program's arguments and its environment. Each function does one thing and takes paths as plain strings. Reach for it in scripts, build tools and small servers. When you need to know why something failed, or to read a big file piece by piece, the fs family (fs for files, fs.dir, fs.path) does more; for building paths use filepath, and for configuration from the environment env. Results follow two conventions. Functions that change something (Remove, Mkdir, Move, Copy, MkdirAll, RemoveAll) return 0 on success and -1 on failure, with no reason attached. WriteFile and AppendFile return the number of bytes written, or -1. Functions that read return an empty value when they fail: ReadFile gives empty text and ListDir an empty vector for a missing folder. There are sub-packages for more specialised work: os.process for running other programs with exact arguments, os.shell for capturing command output and errors, os.platform for identifying the system, os.temp for temporary files, os.user for the current user and os.error for error codes. What people get wrong:  Building a shell command from user input. Run, RunCapture and Output hand the whole string to /bin/sh, so a name such as x; rm -rf ~ runs the second command too. Expecting RunCapture to capture errors. It captures standard output only; the command's standard error still goes to the terminal. Add 2&gt;&amp;1 to the command when you want both. Running Walk on a tree with symbolic links. It follows a link to a folder as if it were a real folder, so it lists what is behind it and never ends on a link loop. RemoveAll does not follow links: it deletes the link itself and leaves its target alone. Using Move across disks. It is a rename, and a rename between file systems fails with -1; copy and remove instead.  The sections below walk through the jobs this package is used for most, each with a complete program and the output it printed. The programs work in the current directory and remove what they create.  Read a settings file with defaults A small program usually keeps its settings in a text file next to it. Exists tells you whether the file is there yet, WriteFile creates it with sensible values the first time, and ReadFile gives you the whole text to pick apart. Every setting that is missing falls back to a default, so an old file keeps working after you add a new option. os-guide-config.salamCopyimport os  func setting(text: str, key: str, fallback: str): str:     each line in text.Split(&quot;
&quot;):         if line.StartsWith(key + &quot;=&quot;):             ret line.Substr(key.Len() + 1, line.Len() - key.Len() - 1)         end     end     ret fallback end  func main:     path := &quot;app.conf&quot;     if not os.Exists(path):         os.WriteFile(path, &quot;port=8080
name=notes
&quot;)         println &quot;wrote a default&quot;, path     end     text := os.ReadFile(path)     println &quot;port:&quot;, setting(text, &quot;port&quot;, &quot;80&quot;)     println &quot;name:&quot;, setting(text, &quot;name&quot;, &quot;app&quot;)     println &quot;debug:&quot;, setting(text, &quot;debug&quot;, &quot;off&quot;)     os.Remove(path) endOutputwrote a default app.conf port: 8080 name: notes debug: off ReadFile returns an empty string both for an empty file and for one it cannot read. When the difference matters, ask Exists first, as here. For a real format, read the text with encoding.json or a similar package instead of splitting lines by hand.  Keep a log and rotate it AppendFile adds to the end of a file and creates it when it is missing, which is all a log needs. FileSize says how big the log has become; once it passes a limit, Move renames it out of the way and the next AppendFile starts a fresh one. os-guide-log.salamCopyimport os  func log(path: str, line: str):     os.AppendFile(path, line + &quot;
&quot;) end  func main:     path := &quot;app.log&quot;     log(path, &quot;started&quot;)     log(path, &quot;user sara signed in&quot;)     log(path, &quot;stopped&quot;)     println &quot;size:&quot;, os.FileSize(path), &quot;bytes&quot;     lines := os.ReadFile(path).Split(&quot;
&quot;)     println &quot;first entry:&quot;, lines.Get(0)     if os.FileSize(path) &gt; 32:         os.Move(path, &quot;app.log.1&quot;)         println &quot;rotated:&quot;, os.Exists(&quot;app.log.1&quot;), os.Exists(path)     end     log(path, &quot;a fresh log&quot;)     println &quot;new size:&quot;, os.FileSize(path)     os.Remove(path)     os.Remove(&quot;app.log.1&quot;) endOutputsize: 36 bytes first entry: started rotated: true false new size: 12 Each call opens and closes the file, which is fine for a few lines a second. For a busy log, open it once with Open(path, "ab") and write to the handle.  Create, walk and remove a directory tree MkdirAll creates a path with every parent it needs, so the order you create folders in does not matter. Walk lists every file below a directory, at any depth, and ListDirs only the folders directly inside it. RemoveAll deletes the whole tree at the end. os-guide-tree.salamCopyimport os import sort  func main:     os.MkdirAll(&quot;site/pages/blog&quot;)     os.MkdirAll(&quot;site/assets&quot;)     os.WriteFile(&quot;site/pages/index.salam&quot;, &quot;func main: end&quot;)     os.WriteFile(&quot;site/pages/blog/first.salam&quot;, &quot;func main: end&quot;)     os.WriteFile(&quot;site/assets/logo.svg&quot;, &quot;&lt;svg/&gt;&quot;)     mut count := 0     mut bytes := 0 as i64     each p in os.Walk(&quot;site&quot;):         if p.EndsWith(&quot;.salam&quot;):             count++             bytes += os.FileSize(p)         end     end     println &quot;source files:&quot;, count, &quot;with&quot;, bytes, &quot;bytes&quot;     folders := os.ListDirs(&quot;site&quot;)     sort.Sort(folders)

net/proxy net.proxy Connecting through an HTTP or SOCKS5 proxy, alone or in a chain. Network A proxy is a middleman that makes the connection on behalf of the program. This package builds the tunnel and gives back an ordinary net.tcp connection, read and written exactly as a direct one is. Two kinds of proxy are supported. An HTTP proxy (NewHTTPProxy, often on port 3128 or 8080) opens the tunnel with a CONNECT request; a SOCKS5 proxy (NewSOCKS5Proxy, port 1080, or 9050 for Tor) uses its own small binary handshake. Both take "host:port", and the ...Auth forms add a user name and password. Dial returns a tcp.Conn; when the proxy cannot be reached or refuses, the connection is empty, so always check it with tcp.ConnOk. What trips people up  The handshake has no timeout. A proxy that accepts the connection but never answers makes Dial wait forever, and there is no way to set a deadline before the handshake. Run the dial in a thread you can give up on if that matters. Nothing is read from the environment. HTTPS_PROXY, ALL_PROXY and NO_PROXY are ignored; read them yourself, as the first guide does. The address must be host:port with a name or an IPv4 address. The text is split at the first colon, so an IPv6 proxy address such as [::1]:1080 is not understood, and a missing port becomes 0. Names are resolved by the proxy. SOCKS5 sends the target as a host name, so the proxy does the DNS lookup. That is what you want with Tor, but it means the target must be reachable by name from the proxy's network. The result is a plain TCP connection. For HTTPS, upgrade it with tcp.UpgradeTLS as the HTTPS guide shows; the package does not do TLS to the proxy itself.   Reading proxy settings from a URL Proxy settings usually arrive as one URL, for example from an HTTPS_PROXY environment variable or a config file: socks5://user:pass@host:1080. The package takes the parts separately, so a program needs a few lines to split the scheme, the credentials and the address. Unknown schemes should be reported rather than silently ignored. net-proxy-guide-url.salamCopyimport net.proxy import str  func from_url(url: str, ok &amp;: bool): proxy.Proxy:     ok = true     mut rest := url     mut socks := false     if str.StartsWith(rest, &quot;socks5://&quot;):         socks = true         rest = rest.Substr(9, rest.Len() - 9)     else str.StartsWith(rest, &quot;http://&quot;):         rest = rest.Substr(7, rest.Len() - 7)     else:         ok = false         ret proxy.Proxy {}     end     mut user := &quot;&quot;     mut pass := &quot;&quot;     at := rest.Find(&quot;@&quot;)     if at &gt;= 0:         cred := rest.Substr(0, at)         rest = rest.Substr(at + 1, rest.Len() - at - 1)         colon := cred.Find(&quot;:&quot;)         user = cred.Substr(0, colon)         pass = cred.Substr(colon + 1, cred.Len() - colon - 1)     end     if socks: ret proxy.NewSOCKS5ProxyAuth(rest, user, pass) end     ret proxy.NewHTTPProxyAuth(rest, user, pass) end  func main:     urls := [&quot;http://10.0.0.5:3128&quot;, &quot;socks5://127.0.0.1:9050&quot;, &quot;socks5://ali:s3cret@gw.example.net:1080&quot;, &quot;ftp://old.example.net:21&quot;]     each u in urls:         mut ok := false         p := from_url(u, ok)         if not ok:             println u, &quot;-&gt; not supported&quot;             continue         end         mut kind := &quot;http&quot;         if p.kind == proxy.TypeSOCKS5: kind = &quot;socks5&quot; end         println u, &quot;-&gt;&quot;, kind, p.host, p.port, &quot;user=[&quot; + p.username + &quot;]&quot;     end endOutputhttp://10.0.0.5:3128 -&gt; http 10.0.0.5 3128 user=[] socks5://127.0.0.1:9050 -&gt; socks5 127.0.0.1 9050 user=[] socks5://ali:s3cret@gw.example.net:1080 -&gt; socks5 gw.example.net 1080 user=[ali] ftp://old.example.net:21 -&gt; not supported  What the tunnel looks like This program runs offline: it starts a tiny fake HTTP proxy on a free local port in a thread, dials through it and prints the request the proxy received. That request is the whole HTTP proxy protocol: a CONNECT host:port line, the Host header, and with credentials a Proxy-Authorization: Basic header holding user:password in Base64. Once the proxy answers 200, every byte is passed straight through, which is why the echo reply comes back on the same connection. net-proxy-guide-local.salamCopyimport net.proxy import net.tcp import str  mut listener := tcp.Server {} mut seen := &quot;&quot;  func fake_proxy():     c := tcp.Accept(listener)     seen = tcp.Read(c, 4096)     tcp.Write(c, &quot;HTTP/1.1 200 Connection established

&quot;)     msg := tcp.Read(c, 100)     tcp.Write(c, &quot;echo: &quot; + msg)     tcp.Close(c) end  func main:     listener = tcp.Bind(0)     worker := spawn(fake_proxy)     p := proxy.NewHTTPProxyAuth(&quot;127.0.0.1:&quot; + str.FromInt(tcp.Port(listener) as i64), &quot;ali&quot;, &quot;secret&quot;)     conn := proxy.Dial(p, &quot;example.com&quot;, 443)     if not tcp.ConnOk(conn):         println &quot;the proxy refused the tunnel&quot;         ret 1     end     tcp.Write(conn, &quot;hello&quot;)     println tcp.Read(conn, 100)     tcp.Close(conn)     join(worker)     print str.Replace(seen, &quot;
&quot;, &quot;
&quot;)     tcp.CloseServer(listener)     ret 0 endOutputecho: hello CONNECT example.com:443 HTTP/1.1 Host: example.com:443 Proxy-Authorization: Basic YWxpOnNlY3JldA== Proxy-Connection: Keep-Alive Basic authentication is only Base64, not encryption. Over an untrusted network, use a proxy you reach through a VPN or SSH tunnel, or send only TLS traffic through it as in the next section.  HTTPS through a proxy A tunnel carries any bytes, including TLS. Dial the proxy for port 443 of the real host, then turn the connection into TLS with tcp.UpgradeTLS and a tls config for that host name. The proxy sees only the host name and encrypted bytes. To try it, start a local proxy such as Squid: docker run -d -p 3128:3128 ubuntu/squid net-proxy-guide-https.salamCopyimport net.proxy import net.tcp impo

net/httpproxy net.httpproxy An HTTP and HTTPS proxy: a client with Basic login and CONNECT tunnels, a server that forwards plain requests and opens tunnels, with access rules, TLS and a connector for proxy chains. Network An HTTP proxy handles two kinds of traffic. A plain request (GET http://example.com/ HTTP/1.1) is forwarded by the proxy, which opens the connection to the website itself. A CONNECT host:port request asks the proxy to open a raw tunnel, and then bytes pass untouched in both directions, which is how HTTPS and any other TCP protocol cross a proxy. This package has both sides: Get, Post, Request and Connect are the client, Start runs a server. It also plugs into net.tunnel as an HttpInbound that can share one port with SOCKS, and as an HttpConnector that is one hop of a proxy chain. To see how the pieces fit together in an app, read Building a v2ray style or VPN app. Core ideas:  Get, Post and Request fetch a plain http:// URL through the proxy and return a Response. For https:// use Connect, which returns a stream, and run TLS over it yourself. Failure is a value. ok and ok are false, with err or status saying why. A 404 or a 407 is a successful round trip with that status. A server is a ServerConfig given to Start. SetConnector or SetDialer decide how targets are reached, so it can be joined to routing rules and proxy chains; allow refuses methods, hosts or ports. The parser is strict on purpose: a request with both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding, two different lengths, folded or malformed headers, a bad method or a head over MaxHeadBytes is refused instead of guessed at. That is how request smuggling is kept out.  Things that catch people out:  A server listens on bind_host, which is 127.0.0.1 by default. Set 0.0.0.0 only on purpose. Without a login, an open HTTP proxy reachable from outside will be used by strangers. Use AddUser, a firewall, or both. A CONNECT proxy can reach internal addresses. A client that may open tunnels to any host and port can reach services on your own network (a database on 10.0.0.5, a metadata address) or send mail on port 25. Restrict it with allow, for example to port 443 and to public hosts. Basic login is not encrypted. The user name and password are Base64, which is only an encoding. Use SetTLS to make the proxy itself speak TLS, or keep it on a trusted link. Bodies are text. body stops at the first NUL byte, and through a HttpInbound a plain request body is limited to 1 MiB of text. Tunnels have no such limit. Only absolute http:// URLs. Request refuses https:// and relative URLs. ParseURL reads both schemes, but only http can go the plain way. HTTP/1.1 only. There is no HTTP/2 and no compressed transfer encoding (a gzip transfer encoding is answered with 501). Close streams returned in stream.   Using an HTTP proxy from a program For a plain website, Get sends the request through the proxy. For anything that is not plain HTTP, and for HTTPS, Connect opens a tunnel and gives you the stream; run TLS or any other protocol over it. The example uses a local proxy and two local servers so that it runs anywhere. net-httpproxy-guide-proxy.salamCopyimport net.httpproxy import net.tcp import sync  mut origin_port := 0 mut echo_port := 0 mut ready := sync.NewWaitGroup()  func origin():     srv := tcp.Bind(0)     origin_port = tcp.Port(srv)     sync.Done(ready)     until true:         c := tcp.Accept(srv)         request := tcp.Read(c, 4096)         first := request.Substr(0, request.Find(&quot;
&quot;))         body := &quot;origin saw: &quot; + first         tcp.Write(c, &quot;HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Length: &quot; + body.Len() + &quot;
Connection: close

&quot; + body)         tcp.Close(c)     end end  func echo():     srv := tcp.Bind(0)     echo_port = tcp.Port(srv)     sync.Done(ready)     until true:         c := tcp.Accept(srv)         tcp.Write(c, &quot;echo:&quot; + tcp.Read(c, 100))         tcp.Close(c)     end end  func start_backends():     sync.Add(ready, 2 as i64)     _a := spawn(origin)     _b := spawn(echo)     sync.Wait(ready) end  func main:     start_backends()     mut server_cfg := httpproxy.NewServerConfig(0)     httpproxy.AllowPrivate(server_cfg, true)     server := httpproxy.Start(server_cfg)     client := httpproxy.NewClient(&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;, server.port)      plain := httpproxy.Get(client, &quot;http://127.0.0.1:&quot; + origin_port + &quot;/news&quot;)     println &quot;plain http:&quot;, plain.status, plain.body      tunnel := httpproxy.Connect(client, &quot;127.0.0.1&quot;, echo_port)     tunnel.stream.Write(&quot;anything, even TLS&quot;)     println &quot;tunnel:&quot;, tunnel.status, tunnel.stream.Read(100)     tunnel.stream.Close()     httpproxy.Stop(server) endOutputplain http: 200 origin saw: GET /news HTTP/1.1 tunnel: 200 echo:anything, even TLS If the proxy needs a login use NewClientAuth; a 407 status means the login was missing or wrong. To use the proxy as one hop of several, see HttpConnector and tunnel.Chain.  Running an HTTP proxy NewServerConfig gives a config for a port, AddUser turns the login on and Start serves in the background. The server keeps connections alive, handles pipelined requests, chunked request bodies, Expect: 100-continue and Upgrade, and strips the headers that belong to one hop (IsHopByHop) before it forwards a request. Slow or silent clients are cut by handshake_timeout_ms and idle_timeout_ms. Decide what the proxy may do before you open it to anyone: allow_connect and allow_plain switch the two kinds of traffic, and allow sees every method, host and port. A proxy for a site should usually allow CONNECT only to port 443 and refuse private addresses. mut config := httpproxy.NewServerConfig(3128) httpproxy.AddUser(config, "alice", "a long random password") config.allow = (method: str, _host: str, port: int): ret port == 443 or method != "CONNECT" end httpproxy.SetTLS(config, "proxy.crt", "proxy.key") server := httpproxy.Start(config) SetTLS turns the proxy int

webpush webpush Sending notifications to browsers from a server, with VAPID keys and the standard encryption. Web and services A web push notification is a message a server sends to a user's browser even when the site is not open: a new chat message, a shipped order, a breaking news alert. This package is the server side. It makes the VAPID keys that identify your server, encrypts each message for one browser under RFC 8291 (aes128gcm) so the push service in between never sees its contents, signs the request, and posts it with net.http. The page side is plain JavaScript: a service worker and pushManager.subscribe. The flow:  Make a key pair once with GenerateKeys and keep it; load it later with KeysFrom. The page subscribes with the public key and sends you the subscription JSON; store it with the user as a Subscription. Send messages with Send, and delete a subscription when the result says gone.  Things that catch people out:  New keys break every subscription. Each subscription is tied to the public key it was made with. A ttl of 0 is replaced by the 28 day default, so "deliver now or drop it" cannot be expressed; use a short value such as 60 instead. The payload size is not checked. More than 3993 bytes of text is still encrypted and sent, and the push service rejects it, usually with 413. Send waits as long as the push service takes; it has no time limit, so sending to thousands of users one after another can be slow. KeysFrom only checks that both keys are non-empty.   Make the VAPID keys once The VAPID key pair identifies your server to the push services, and every browser subscription is tied to its public key. If you make new keys at every start, all existing subscriptions stop working. So make them once, keep them somewhere safe, and load them with KeysFrom afterwards. The program keeps them in a file; a real server would more likely read them from its configuration or environment. webpush-keys-file.salamCopyimport webpush import os import str  func load_or_create(path: str): webpush.Keys:     if os.Exists(path):         lines := str.Split(os.ReadFile(path), &quot;
&quot;)         if lines.Len() &gt;= 2:             ret webpush.KeysFrom(lines.Get(0), lines.Get(1))         end     end     fresh := webpush.GenerateKeys()     os.WriteFile(path, fresh.public_key + &quot;
&quot; + fresh.private_key + &quot;
&quot;)     ret fresh end  func main:     path := &quot;vapid-keys.txt&quot;     first := load_or_create(path)     second := load_or_create(path)     println &quot;keys valid:&quot;, first.ok, second.ok     println &quot;same keys on the second start:&quot;, first.public_key == second.public_key     println &quot;public key length:&quot;, first.public_key.Len()     println &quot;uncompressed point (starts with B):&quot;, str.StartsWith(first.public_key, &quot;B&quot;)     os.Remove(path) endOutputkeys valid: true true same keys on the second start: true public key length: 87 uncompressed point (starts with B): true The public key is the 87 character text that the page passes as applicationServerKey to pushManager.subscribe. The private key never leaves the server.  Read the subscription the browser sends After the user allows notifications, pushManager.subscribe() in the page returns a subscription. Its toJSON() form has an endpoint and two keys, p256dh and auth. Send that JSON to your server, read it into a Subscription with encoding.json and store it with the user. Encrypt reports through its ok argument whether the keys are usable, which is a cheap way to reject a broken subscription before you store it. webpush-subscription.salamCopyimport webpush import encoding.json import str  func from_browser(text: str): webpush.Subscription:     ret webpush.Subscription {         endpoint = json.Get(text, &quot;endpoint&quot;),         p256dh = json.GetPath(text, &quot;keys.p256dh&quot;),         auth = json.GetPath(text, &quot;keys.auth&quot;)     } end  func main:     posted := `{&quot;endpoint&quot;:&quot;https://fcm.googleapis.com/fcm/send/dQw4w9WgXcQ:APA91b&quot;,&quot;expirationTime&quot;:null,&quot;keys&quot;:{&quot;p256dh&quot;:&quot;BCVxsr7N_eNgVRqvHtD0zTZsEc6-VV-JvLexhqUzORcxaOzi6-AYWXvTBHm4bjyPjs7Vd8pZGH6SRpkNtoIAiw4&quot;,&quot;auth&quot;:&quot;BTBZMqHH6r4Tts7J_aSIgg&quot;}}`     sub := from_browser(posted)     println &quot;endpoint:&quot;, sub.endpoint     pr