ssl ssl A familiar OpenSSL style interface for secure connections, on top of Salam's own TLS. Security and cryptography This package gives an OpenSSL style context and connection interface (SSL_CTX, SSL_connect, SSL_read and friends, under Salam names), but underneath it is Salam's own tls and it needs no outside library. Use it when you port C code written against OpenSSL, or when you like that split between a shared context and many connections. For new code tls is shorter, and for web requests net.http does all of it for you. The flow: make one context, hand each TCP socket to it with Wrap, for a client set the hostname and call Connect (a server calls Accept), then Read and Write. Things to know:  A client context checks certificates against the system roots from the start, with TLS 1.2 as the minimum. LoadVerifyLocationsFile replaces the system roots with your file; SetDefaultVerifyPaths switches back. SetHostname is required before Connect while verification is on; without it the handshake is refused. Shutdown and Close do not close the socket. As in OpenSSL, the TCP connection you wrapped is yours; close it with net.tcp afterwards. SetMinVersion(ctx, TLS1_3) is checked after the handshake: a server that only offers 1.2 is connected and then refused. Read returns a str, which stops at a zero byte.   A complete client The steps are always the same: one context for the whole program, then for each connection a TCP socket, Wrap, SetHostname and Connect. A context made with NewClientContext already trusts the system roots and stops the handshake when the check fails; the calls to SetDefaultVerifyPaths and SetVerify(ctx, VERIFY_PEER) in the program only make that explicit, the way OpenSSL code usually reads. Read returns an empty string when the server closes the connection, which is the end of a Connection: close response. ssl-fetch-page.salamCopyimport ssl import net.tcp import str  func fetch(ctx: ssl.Context, host: str, path: str): str:     sock := tcp.Dial(hos