gui/webview/bundle gui.webview.bundle What salam bundle runs to make installers. Graphics and games This package is what runs when you type salam bundle. It reads the [app] table of salam.toml, builds the program, packs its assets and makes installers. See making installers for how to use it.  The [app] table Everything salam bundle needs is in the [app] table of salam.toml. Only id and name matter much: the version defaults to 0.1.0, the entry file to main.salam and the category to Utility. slug is made from the name for file names. ReadManifest returns false when there is no table. gui-webview-bundle-guide-manifest.salamCopyimport gui.webview.bundle import io  func main:     text := &quot;[app]
id = \&quot;com.example.notes\&quot;
name = \&quot;Notes\&quot;
version = \&quot;1.2.0\&quot;
assets = \&quot;www\&quot;
category = \&quot;Utility\&quot;
&quot;     io.WriteFile(&quot;salam.toml&quot;, text)     mut m := bundle.Manifest {}     if not bundle.ReadManifest(&quot;salam.toml&quot;, m):         println &quot;no [app] table&quot;         ret 1     end     println m.id     println m.name, m.version     println &quot;main file:&quot;, m.main     println &quot;assets:&quot;, m.assets     println &quot;file name:&quot;, m.slug endOutputcom.example.notes Notes 1.2.0 main file: main.salam assets: www file name: notes  Formats and targets The default formats are tar and deb on Linux, zip on Windows, and app and tar on macOS. Name others with --format; each needs its tool on the PATH. $ salam bundle --format=rpm,appimage      # rpmbuild, appimagetool $ salam bundle --target=x86_64-w64-windows-gnu --format=nsis   # makensis $ salam bundle --target=arm64-apple-macos --format=app,dmg     # hdiutil $ salam bundle --output=dist A cross build needs no extra software, because the compiler links Windows and macOS programs itself. A PNG icon is converted to .ico and .icns. Nothing is signed, so sign and notarize before you hand a program to other people.  The web engine is part of the [app] table too. By default a program uses the web view of the operating system. Set engine = "chromium", or pass --engine=chromium, to build against Chromium Embedded Framework, and point runtime (or --runtime=DIR, or the SALAM_CEF_DIR environment variable) at the folder with the built salam_webview_cef library, salam_cef_helper and the CEF runtime files. That folder is copied next to the program, and only for tar on Linux and zip on Windows. See choosing the web engine for what the Chromium engine can do today.  Building installers in CI Run the same command on one Linux runner for every system, passing a different --target and --output each time, and upload the results. The desktop.yml workflow in the Salam repository does this and then runs each result on a real Windows, macOS and Linux machine. Types Manifeststruct ManifestThe [app] table of salam.toml.NameTypeDescriptionidstr = ""The application id.namestr = ""The name people see.versionstr = "0.1.0"The version.mainstr = "main.salam"The entry file, main.salam by default.assetsstr = ""The folder packed into the application.iconstr = ""A PNG icon; it is converted for each system.descriptionstr = ""One line about the application.publisherstr = ""Who publishes it.categorystr = "Utility"The desktop menu category.slugstr = ""The file name made from the name.enginestr = "system"The web engine of the program: system (the web view of the operating system, the default) or chromium.runtimestr = ""The folder with the Chromium runtime files, used with the chromium engine.gui-webview-bundle-Main.salamCopyimport gui.webview.bundle import os  func main:     mut m := bundle.Manifest {         id = &quot;com.example.app&quot;, name = &quot;App&quot;, version = &quot;1.0&quot;, main = &quot;main.salam&quot;, assets = &quot;www&quot;, icon = &quot;icon.png&quot;, description = &quot;An app&quot;, publisher = &quot;Example&quot;, category = &quot;Utility&quot;, slug = &quot;app&quot;, engine = &quot;system&quot;, runtime = &quot;&quot;     }     println bundle.ReadManifest(&quot;salam.toml&quot;, m), m.name     ret bundle.Main(os.Args()) end Functions ReadManifestfunc ReadManifest(path: str, out &amp;: Manifest): boolReads the [app] table of a file.The example for this item is shown with the ones above: gui-webview-bundle-Main.salam Mainfunc Main(args: Vector&lt;str&gt;): intWhat salam bundle runs. Returns the exit code.The example for this item is shown with the ones above: gui-webview-bundle-Main.salam
